===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pleh₁-}}
The {{glossary|adjective}} and {{glossary|noun}} are derived from Late {{inh|en|enm|publik}}, {{m|enm|publike|t=(''adjective'') generally observable, public; relating to the general public or public affairs; (''noun'') a generally observable place or situation}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=pū̆blik(e|pos=adj|id=MED35134}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|public}}, {{m|xno|publik}}, {{m|xno|publique}}, {{der|en|frm|public}}, {{m|frm|publique}}, and {{der|en|fro|public|t=(''adjective'') generally observable, public; relating to the general public; official; (''noun'') community or its members collectively; nation, state; audience, spectators collectively}} (modern {{cog|fr|public}}, {{m|fr|publique}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}); and from their {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la|pūblicus|t=of or belonging to the community, people, or state; general, public}}, an alteration of {{m|la|poplicus}} (influenced by {{m|la|pūbēs|t=adult men; male population}}), from {{m|la|poplus|t=community; the people, public; nation, state}} (later {{m|la|populus}}; from {{der|en|itc-pro|*poplos|t=army}}; further origin uncertain, possibly from {{der|en|ett|-}} or from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pleh₁-|t=to fill}}) + {{m|la|-icus|pos={{glossary|suffix}} meaning ‘of or pertaining to’}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=adj.'' and ''n|id=5287184156|date=June 2024|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj.'' and ''n|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220704124559/https://www.lexico.com/definition/public}}</ref> Related to {{m|en|people}}, {{m|en|populus}}{{,}} etc.

The Middle English word displaced native {{noncog|ang|ceorlfolc}} and {{m|ang|folclic}}.

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the adjective.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=4991342587|date=July 2023}}</ref>

